export const EXPLORING_CONTENT = {
  ex1l1: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `What biology actually studies`, body: `Biology is the science of living things, and it spans a huge range of scale — from single molecules inside a cell, up through organisms, all the way to entire ecosystems. What ties all of it together are a handful of unifying themes that show up again and again: living things are built from cells, they pass information to their offspring through DNA, they capture and use energy to stay alive, and they change over generations through evolution. Every other topic in biology — anatomy, genetics, ecology, microbiology — is really just a closer look at one of these themes. Once you have this framework, new biology content stops feeling like a pile of disconnected facts and starts feeling like variations on a small set of ideas.` },
        { heading: `The cell as the basic unit of life`, body: `Cell theory states that all living things are made of one or more cells, and that the cell is the smallest unit that can carry out all the functions of life. Some organisms (like bacteria) are single prokaryotic cells with no nucleus — their DNA just floats in the cytoplasm. Your body, by contrast, is built from trillions of eukaryotic cells, each with a nucleus housing its DNA and specialized organelles doing specific jobs: mitochondria generate energy, ribosomes build proteins, and the cell membrane controls what gets in and out. Almost everything that goes wrong in disease — an infection, a cancer, a genetic disorder — is ultimately something going wrong at the cellular level first.` },
        { heading: `How energy moves through living systems`, body: `Every living thing needs a constant supply of usable energy, and almost all of that energy on Earth traces back to sunlight. Plants and other producers capture light energy through photosynthesis and store it in glucose. Then, through cellular respiration, cells (in plants, animals, and virtually everything else) break that glucose down and use the energy to build ATP — the molecule your cells actually spend to power everything from muscle contraction to nerve signaling to simply keeping your organs running. Photosynthesis and respiration are essentially mirror images of each other, and understanding that relationship is one of the most useful shortcuts in all of intro biology.` },
        { heading: `DNA: the instruction manual`, body: `DNA is a long molecule made of four repeating building blocks (A, T, C, G) whose exact sequence encodes the instructions for building and running an organism. Segments of DNA called genes carry the instructions for specific traits or proteins, and DNA gets copied and passed down every time a cell divides or an organism reproduces — which is how traits get passed from parents to offspring. The fact that DNA is both stable enough to preserve information reliably and flexible enough to occasionally mutate is exactly what makes both heredity and evolution possible at the same time.` },
        { heading: `Why this matters for a health career`, body: `Whichever health career you end up in — physician, nurse, pharmacist, PT, researcher — you'll be reasoning about cells, energy, and genetics constantly, just applied differently. A nurse thinks about cellular oxygen delivery when reading a patient's vitals. A pharmacist thinks about how a drug's molecule interacts with a specific protein. A researcher thinks about how a mutation changes a protein's function. This lesson isn't background trivia — it's the shared vocabulary every one of those conversations is built on.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `All living things are made of cells, and the cell is the smallest unit that can carry out every function of life.`,
        `Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are mirror-image processes that move energy through nearly every living system.`,
        `DNA's sequence encodes the instructions for building and running an organism, and gets copied every time a cell divides.`,
        `These same few themes — cells, energy, information, evolution — underlie every health career, not just research or medicine.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: 'tZE_fQFK8EY', title: 'Introduction to Biology: Crash Course Biology #1', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },
  ex1l2: {
    readMins: 7,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `From basics to AP-level depth`, body: `Once you're comfortable with the fundamentals — cells, energy, DNA — the next step is going one level deeper into the molecular mechanics behind heredity: how DNA actually gets copied, how traits actually get inherited, and where new variation actually comes from. This is the level of detail AP Biology (and eventually intro college biology) expects, and getting comfortable with it now means you're not starting from scratch when that class starts moving fast.` },
        { heading: `DNA replication in brief`, body: `Before a cell divides, it has to copy its entire DNA so each new cell gets a full set of instructions. This process, called DNA replication, is semi-conservative — meaning each new DNA molecule ends up with one original ("parent") strand and one brand-new strand. An enzyme called helicase unwinds and separates the two original strands, and another enzyme, DNA polymerase, reads each separated strand and builds a matching new strand alongside it, base by base (A pairs with T, C pairs with G). The result is two DNA molecules that are each identical to the original — a remarkably reliable copying process, though not perfect, which matters for the next section.` },
        { heading: `Genes, alleles, and inheritance`, body: `A gene is a specific segment of DNA that codes for a particular trait, and most genes come in different versions called alleles — for example, different alleles of a gene might produce different eye colors. You inherit one copy of most genes from each parent, and if those two copies (alleles) differ, one may be dominant (its effect shows up) while the other is recessive (its effect is masked unless you have two recessive copies). A Punnett square is just a simple grid for predicting what combinations of alleles — and therefore what traits — offspring are likely to inherit from two parents with known genotypes.` },
        { heading: `Mutations and variation`, body: `DNA replication is extremely accurate, but not flawless — occasionally a base gets copied incorrectly, creating a mutation. Most mutations are neutral or even harmful, but every so often one turns out to be beneficial in a given environment. This is the raw material evolution actually works with: without mutations creating new variation in the first place, natural selection would have nothing new to act on. Mutations are also the reason no two people (identical twins aside) have exactly the same DNA, and why genetic diseases and drug responses can vary meaningfully between individuals.` },
        { heading: `Why this depth pays off before you even take the AP exam`, body: `Building real fluency with replication, inheritance, and mutation now — rather than cramming it right before a test — is exactly the kind of foundation that makes AP Biology, college intro biology, and eventually anything MCAT-adjacent feel like reinforcement instead of a first encounter. This is deliberately the same level of depth pre-med, pre-nursing, and pre-research students all eventually need, regardless of which specific health career you land on.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `DNA replication is semi-conservative: each new DNA molecule keeps one original strand and builds one new one.`,
        `Alleles are different versions of a gene; dominant alleles mask recessive ones when both are present.`,
        `Punnett squares predict the probability of specific trait combinations in offspring based on parental genotypes.`,
        `Mutations are copying errors that create genetic variation — the raw material both evolution and individual differences in drug response rely on.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: '8kK2zwjRV0M', title: 'DNA Structure and Replication: Crash Course Biology #10', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },
  ex1l3: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Anatomy vs. physiology`, body: `Anatomy is the study of structure — what the body's parts are and how they're arranged. Physiology is the study of function — how those parts actually work, individually and together, to keep you alive. The two are inseparable in practice: you can't really understand why the heart's four chambers are shaped and positioned the way they are without understanding what physiological job each one does. Every health career blends both — a nurse needs to know where a vein is (anatomy) and why blood pressure changes the way it does (physiology).` },
        { heading: `Homeostasis: the central theme`, body: `Homeostasis is your body's drive to keep its internal environment stable — temperature, blood sugar, pH, hydration — despite a constantly changing external world. It mostly works through negative feedback loops: a sensor detects a change, a control center compares it to a set point, and an effector responds to push things back toward normal. When you're cold, your body shivers to generate heat; when blood sugar rises after a meal, insulin brings it back down. Nearly every physiological system you'll ever study is, at some level, a mechanism serving this one underlying goal.` },
        { heading: `A whirlwind tour of major systems`, body: `The circulatory system moves blood, oxygen, and nutrients throughout the body. The respiratory system handles gas exchange — oxygen in, carbon dioxide out. The nervous system sends fast electrical signals for control and coordination, while the endocrine system sends slower chemical signals (hormones) for longer-term regulation. The digestive system breaks food down into usable nutrients, and the excretory system filters waste out of the blood. No system works in isolation — they're constantly communicating and depending on each other.` },
        { heading: `How organ systems work together`, body: `Take something as simple as going for a run: your nervous system signals your muscles to contract, your circulatory system speeds up to deliver more oxygen to those muscles, your respiratory system increases its rate to supply that oxygen and clear out extra carbon dioxide, and your endocrine system releases hormones to mobilize energy stores. A single activity recruits nearly every organ system simultaneously — which is exactly why physiology is usually taught system by system but always needs to be understood as one integrated whole.` },
        { heading: `Why physiology is the shared language of every health career`, body: `Whether you become a physician, nurse, PT, or pharmacist, physiology is the common language everyone on a care team uses to describe what's actually happening in a patient's body. A physician orders a treatment based on physiological reasoning, a nurse monitors physiological signs of how a patient is responding, and a PT designs an exercise plan around physiological recovery. Getting comfortable with this material now means you'll be able to actually follow (and eventually contribute to) that conversation.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Anatomy is structure; physiology is function — and health careers require fluency in both together.`,
        `Homeostasis (keeping the internal environment stable via negative feedback) is the unifying goal behind most physiological systems.`,
        `The body's major systems — circulatory, respiratory, nervous, endocrine, digestive — constantly communicate rather than working in isolation.`,
        `Physiology is the shared vocabulary every health career uses to describe what's happening inside a patient.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: 'uBGl2BujkPQ', title: 'Introduction to Anatomy & Physiology: Crash Course A&P #1', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },
  ex2l1: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Why medicine needs chemistry`, body: `It's easy to think of chemistry as a subject separate from medicine, but almost everything in healthcare is chemistry applied to the human body. A blood test measures the concentration of specific molecules. A drug works by binding to a specific receptor, a chemical interaction with a specific shape and charge requirement. Even something as basic as why your body needs a stable pH, or how anesthesia actually works, comes down to chemistry. This lesson covers the fundamentals that every deeper health-science topic — pharmacology, biochemistry, physiology — eventually builds on.` },
        { heading: `Atoms and the periodic table`, body: `Every atom has a nucleus (protons and neutrons) surrounded by electrons, and an element's identity is determined entirely by its number of protons. The periodic table organizes elements by their properties, and those properties follow predictable trends: atomic size generally shrinks moving left to right across a period (as nuclear charge increases and pulls electrons in tighter) and grows moving down a group (as additional electron shells are added). Understanding these trends lets you predict a lot about how an element will behave chemically just from its position on the table.` },
        { heading: `How atoms bond`, body: `Atoms bond to become more stable, typically by achieving a full outer electron shell. In ionic bonding, one atom transfers electrons to another, creating oppositely charged ions that attract each other (like sodium and chloride in table salt). In covalent bonding, atoms share electrons instead of transferring them — this is how most of the molecules in your body, including water, are built. Water's particular bonding pattern gives it a slightly charged (polar) shape, which is exactly why it's such an effective solvent for the countless reactions happening inside your cells at any given moment.` },
        { heading: `Reactions and equilibrium`, body: `A chemical reaction transforms reactants into products by breaking and forming bonds, and a balanced chemical equation just confirms that atoms aren't created or destroyed in the process — everything on one side has to be accounted for on the other. Many biologically important reactions are reversible and reach a state of equilibrium, where the forward and reverse reactions happen at the same rate. Your blood's pH buffering system is a real-world example: it constantly shifts a chemical equilibrium to keep your blood's pH in an extremely narrow, life-sustaining range.` },
        { heading: `Chemistry as the language underneath biology`, body: `Biochemistry — the chemistry of living things — is really just regular chemistry applied to biological molecules: proteins, DNA, carbohydrates, lipids. Once you're solid on atomic structure, bonding, and basic reactions, biology topics that seemed abstract (like enzyme function or how a hormone triggers a response) start making a lot more literal, mechanical sense. That's exactly why chemistry fundamentals are considered a prerequisite, not an elective, for essentially every health career path.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `An element's identity comes from its number of protons; the periodic table's layout reflects predictable trends in atomic properties.`,
        `Ionic bonds form through electron transfer; covalent bonds form through electron sharing — most molecules in your body are covalent.`,
        `Balanced equations conserve atoms across a reaction, and many biological reactions exist in a dynamic, adjustable equilibrium.`,
        `Chemistry fundamentals are the mechanical basis for biochemistry, pharmacology, and physiology — not a separate, unrelated subject.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: 'FSyAehMdpyI', title: 'The Nucleus: Crash Course Chemistry #1', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },
  ex2l2: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Why physics matters in health careers`, body: `Physics might seem like the least "medical" of the sciences, but it shows up constantly in healthcare: X-rays and MRIs are applications of electromagnetic physics, blood pressure and blood flow are fluid dynamics, hearing relies on the physics of sound waves, and physical therapy is essentially applied biomechanics. Even understanding why a cast or brace is shaped a certain way draws on basic mechanics. This lesson covers the physics fundamentals that keep showing up, in slightly different clothing, across nearly every health field.` },
        { heading: `Motion basics`, body: `Speed describes how fast something is moving; velocity adds direction to that (a car going 60 mph north has a different velocity than one going 60 mph south, even with the same speed). Acceleration describes how quickly velocity itself is changing — speeding up, slowing down, or changing direction all count as acceleration. These three ideas are the foundation for describing any kind of movement precisely, whether it's a thrown ball, a heart valve closing, or blood moving through a vessel.` },
        { heading: `Forces and Newton's laws`, body: `Newton's first law says an object in motion stays in motion (and one at rest stays at rest) unless a force acts on it. His second law, F = ma, says the force needed to accelerate an object depends on its mass — a useful idea for understanding everything from how much force a joint experiences during a sprint to how a defibrillator's mechanical design works. His third law says every force has an equal and opposite reaction force, which is literally why walking works: you push backward on the ground, and it pushes you forward.` },
        { heading: `Energy and work`, body: `In physics, work is done whenever a force moves an object over a distance, and energy is the capacity to do that work. The law of conservation of energy says energy can't be created or destroyed, only converted from one form to another — chemical energy in food becomes kinetic energy in your muscles, for example. This single idea underlies an enormous range of biological and clinical topics, from metabolism to how a pacemaker's battery is designed to last.` },
        { heading: `Where physics shows up in the body`, body: `Blood pressure is literally a measure of the force fluid exerts on vessel walls, and blood flow follows the same fluid-dynamics principles as water through a pipe — which is why a narrowed artery (like in atherosclerosis) so dramatically changes flow and pressure. Physical therapists reason constantly in terms of forces, torque, and leverage around joints. Even basic imaging technology depends on physics: X-rays pass through soft tissue but are absorbed by dense bone, which is what creates the image.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Speed, velocity, and acceleration precisely describe motion — the vocabulary underlying everything from biomechanics to blood flow.`,
        `Newton's three laws explain how forces affect the body, from joint loading during movement to the mechanics of walking itself.`,
        `Energy is conserved, not created or destroyed — the same principle underlying metabolism and medical device design.`,
        `Blood pressure, blood flow, imaging technology, and physical therapy are all direct, everyday applications of basic physics.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: 'ZM8ECpBuQYE', title: 'Motion in a Straight Line: Crash Course Physics #1', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },
  ex2l3: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Why every health career needs statistics literacy`, body: `Whether you're reading about a new drug's clinical trial results, deciding how much weight to put on a single lab value, or just trying to figure out whether a health headline is overblown, you're relying on statistics literacy. Health careers run on data — comparing treatments, tracking outcomes, identifying risk factors — and being able to read that data critically (rather than just accepting a headline's framing) is a skill that pays off in every single health career, not just research.` },
        { heading: `Describing data`, body: `The mean (average), median (middle value), and mode (most frequent value) each summarize a data set differently, and which one is most useful depends on the data. The mean is sensitive to extreme outliers — a handful of very high values can drag it upward — while the median isn't, which is why household income is usually reported as a median. Spread (how much data varies, often measured by standard deviation) matters just as much as the center: two treatments could have the same average outcome but wildly different consistency.` },
        { heading: `Probability basics`, body: `Probability measures how likely an event is, expressed as a number between 0 (impossible) and 1 (certain). In a health context, this shows up constantly: the probability a screening test correctly flags a disease, the probability a given side effect occurs, the probability a treatment outperforms a placebo purely by chance. Understanding basic probability is what lets you tell the difference between "this happened" and "this was likely to happen anyway even if the treatment did nothing."` },
        { heading: `Why "statistically significant" doesn't mean "big effect"`, body: `A result being statistically significant just means it's unlikely to have occurred by random chance alone — it says nothing about how large or clinically meaningful the effect actually is. A drug trial can find a statistically significant improvement that amounts to a tiny, practically irrelevant difference, especially with a very large sample size. Learning to ask "how big is the effect, not just whether it's real" is one of the most useful critical-thinking habits in all of health science.` },
        { heading: `Correlation isn't causation`, body: `Two things being correlated (moving together) doesn't prove one causes the other — there could be a third factor driving both, or the relationship could be coincidental. Ice cream sales and drowning rates are correlated, for instance, because both rise in summer heat, not because ice cream causes drowning. This distinction matters enormously in health research, where a lot of early findings show correlation and it takes carefully controlled studies to establish actual causation.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Mean, median, and mode each summarize data differently, and outliers can distort the mean much more than the median.`,
        `Probability measures likelihood on a 0-to-1 scale, and underlies how screening tests and treatment effects are evaluated.`,
        `"Statistically significant" means "unlikely to be chance" — it does not automatically mean "a large or important effect."`,
        `Correlation between two variables never proves that one causes the other; controlled studies are what establish causation.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: 'sxQaBpKfDRk', title: 'What Is Statistics: Crash Course Statistics #1', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },
  ex3l1: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `What psychology actually is`, body: `Psychology is the scientific study of the mind and behavior — not just "understanding people" informally, but using the same kind of controlled observation, hypothesis testing, and data analysis used in any other science. It asks questions at every level, from how individual neurons fire to how entire groups of people behave under social pressure. For a health career, psychology isn't a "soft" add-on subject — it's the science of why patients behave the way they do, why they do or don't follow treatment plans, and how to communicate with someone who is scared, in pain, or confused.` },
        { heading: `Major perspectives`, body: `Psychology looks at behavior through several different lenses that complement rather than compete with each other. The biological perspective looks at brain structures, neurotransmitters, and genetics. The cognitive perspective focuses on mental processes like memory, perception, and decision-making. The behavioral perspective focuses on how behavior is shaped by reinforcement and environment. And the humanistic perspective emphasizes personal growth and individual experience. A real understanding of any behavior usually draws on more than one of these at once.` },
        { heading: `Psychology's place in health careers`, body: `Every single patient interaction has a psychological dimension: how you explain a diagnosis affects whether it's understood and believed, how you deliver bad news affects how it's processed, and understanding motivation is often the difference between a treatment plan that gets followed and one that doesn't. Fields as seemingly non-psychological as surgery and pharmacy still depend on understanding patient behavior, informed consent, and communication — psychology isn't reserved for mental-health-specific careers.` },
        { heading: `Nature vs. nurture`, body: `One of psychology's oldest questions is how much of a given trait or behavior comes from genetics (nature) versus environment and experience (nurture). Modern psychology has mostly moved past a strict either/or framing — most traits emerge from a constant interaction between the two, not one winning out over the other. This framing matters clinically too: a patient's health behaviors are shaped by both biology and their life circumstances, and effective care usually has to address both.` },
        { heading: `Why this matters even if you're not going into mental health`, body: `You don't need to become a psychiatrist or therapist for this content to matter. A nurse manages patient anxiety before a procedure. A physician explains a scary diagnosis in a way that's actually heard. A PT keeps a patient motivated through a slow, sometimes discouraging recovery. Every one of those moments is applied psychology, whether or not the job title says so.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Psychology is the scientific study of mind and behavior, using the same evidence-based methods as any other science.`,
        `Biological, cognitive, behavioral, and humanistic perspectives each explain a different piece of why people behave as they do.`,
        `Effective patient communication, motivation, and treatment adherence are all fundamentally psychological skills, not just clinical ones.`,
        `The nature-vs-nurture debate is really about interaction, not competition — both genetics and environment shape health behavior.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: 'vo4pMVb0R6M', title: 'Intro to Psychology: Crash Course Psychology #1', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },
  ex3l2: {
    readMins: 5,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `What shadowing actually is (and isn't)`, body: `Shadowing means observing a clinician as they go about their normal work — you're not examining patients, making decisions, or touching equipment. It's the most realistic way for a high schooler to see what a health career actually looks like day to day, beyond what TV medical dramas or a job description can tell you: the pacing, the paperwork, the hard conversations, and the parts nobody puts in a brochure. It's observation, not participation, and that distinction matters for how you should behave during it.` },
        { heading: `How to realistically arrange it as a high schooler`, body: `The most realistic path into shadowing at your age is almost always through a personal connection — a family physician, a relative in healthcare, or a family friend — rather than a cold application to a hospital. Hospitals rarely accept unsolicited shadowing requests from minors directly, partly due to liability and patient-privacy concerns, so a parent or guardian is usually involved in setting up the arrangement. If you don't have an obvious connection, it's worth asking your school counselor, teachers, or even your own doctor whether they know someone open to it.` },
        { heading: `What to actually pay attention to`, body: `It's tempting to only notice the dramatic moments, but the most useful thing to observe is the ordinary, repeated rhythm of the job: How does the clinician talk to patients? How do they handle uncertainty or a difficult case? How much of the day is direct patient contact versus documentation and coordination? These are the details that actually tell you whether a career fits your personality and interests, far more than any single dramatic case will.` },
        { heading: `Professionalism and confidentiality`, body: `Whatever you observe about a specific patient stays confidential — no discussing identifiable details with friends, and absolutely nothing on social media. This is a hard boundary, not a suggestion, and it's the reason many shadowing arrangements come with an explicit confidentiality agreement to sign. Dress professionally, show up on time, and treat the day as a guest in someone else's workplace, because in a very real sense, that's exactly what you are.` },
        { heading: `Turning a shadowing day into something you can use later`, body: `Right after a shadowing day, log the date, setting, specialty, and your own reflections in your Portfolio's Clinical Hours section — never patient-identifying details, just your own observations and what you learned. Building this record as you go, rather than trying to reconstruct it months later, is what makes a shadowing history genuinely credible when it eventually matters for an application.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Shadowing is observation, not participation — you're there to watch and learn, not to practice or make decisions.`,
        `Realistic access as a minor almost always comes through a personal connection with a parent or guardian involved.`,
        `The ordinary, repeated details of a workday reveal more about career fit than any single dramatic moment.`,
        `Confidentiality is a hard rule, not a suggestion — log your own reflections, never identifiable patient details.`,
      ],
    },
    // No video for this lesson — no single canonical, verifiable YouTube video exists
    // for "how to shadow a doctor as a high schooler"; the LessonPlayer skips the
    // video step gracefully when `video` is absent.
  },
  ex3l3: {
    readMins: 5,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Why "pre-health" isn't a single track`, body: `"Pre-health" describes a foundation, not a commitment to one specific career. Physician, nurse, PT, pharmacist, and researcher tracks all draw on the same core: strong science coursework, real (age-appropriate) exposure to the field, and an honest sense of what the day-to-day work actually involves. You genuinely don't need to have picked a specific health career yet to make real progress right now — the foundation is shared, and the specific choice can come later.` },
        { heading: `What programs actually screen for at this stage`, body: `At the college-application stage, admissions readers for pre-health-friendly programs are mostly looking for strong science grades, genuine curiosity (not just a resume line), and evidence of sustained interest rather than a single one-off activity. A few months of consistent shadowing or volunteering tells a much stronger story than a two-week trip framed as "healthcare experience." Depth and consistency beat a long list of shallow activities almost every time.` },
        { heading: `The value of staying undeclared a little longer`, body: `It's genuinely fine — often smart — to stay undeclared on a specific health career for a while. Sampling physician-track content, nursing-track content, and research-track content lets you make a much more informed choice later than committing early based on limited information. The diagnostic in this app exists exactly for this reason: to help you sample broadly and re-check your fit as your interests sharpen, not to lock you into a decision on day one.` },
        { heading: `Building a foundation that works no matter which path you pick`, body: `Biology, chemistry, physics, statistics, and basic psychology aren't specific to one health career — they're the shared foundation underneath all of them. Time spent getting genuinely strong in these fundamentals now is never wasted, regardless of which specific pathway you eventually land on. This is exactly why the Exploring Pre-Health pathway is built the way it is: broad enough to keep every door open, rigorous enough that nothing here needs to be relearned later.` },
        { heading: `What to do next`, body: `Keep logging any shadowing, volunteering, or coursework experience as you go. Retake the diagnostic every few months — your answers (and your fit) will shift as you learn more about yourself and about different health careers. And don't feel pressure to have this fully figured out in high school; very few people do, and the ones who claim they did usually revised the plan anyway once they got more real exposure to the field.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Pre-health is a shared foundation across many careers, not a commitment to one specific path.`,
        `Sustained, consistent experience matters far more to admissions readers than a long list of one-off activities.`,
        `Staying undeclared while you sample different pathways is a legitimate, often smart strategy — not indecision.`,
        `Science fundamentals built now transfer to every health career, so nothing here is wasted regardless of what you choose later.`,
      ],
    },
    // No video for this lesson — same reasoning as ex3l2 above.
  },

  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // UNIT 4 — THE HUMAN BODY, SYSTEM BY SYSTEM
  // Written for a student who hasn't picked a health career yet: the same organ
  // systems the specialized tracks cover, but framed as shared literacy every
  // health path assumes rather than as preparation for one specific role.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ex4l1: {
    readMins: 7,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `One system, two halves`, body: `The circulatory and respiratory systems are usually taught separately and function as a single delivery operation. The respiratory system's job is gas exchange: getting oxygen out of the air and into the blood, and getting carbon dioxide out of the blood and into the air. The circulatory system's job is transport: moving that oxygenated blood to every tissue and carrying waste back. Neither is useful without the other, which is why almost any problem in one shows up as a symptom that could equally point to the other. Shortness of breath is the standard example — it might be a lung problem, a heart problem, an anemia problem, or an anxiety problem, and telling those apart is a skill essentially every health career needs some version of.` },
        { heading: `How the heart actually moves blood`, body: `The heart is functionally two pumps sharing a beat. The right side takes oxygen-poor blood returning from the body and pushes it a short distance to the lungs. The left side takes freshly oxygenated blood from the lungs and drives it out to the whole body — which is why the left ventricle's muscular wall is much thicker. Each beat has a contraction phase (systole) and a relaxation-and-filling phase (diastole), and one-way valves keep blood moving in one direction. A blood pressure reading of 118/76 reports the peak pressure during contraction over the pressure remaining during relaxation, and under 120/80 is considered normal under current US guidelines.` },
        { heading: `Gas exchange in the alveoli`, body: `Air travels down the trachea, through progressively branching bronchi and bronchioles, and arrives at the alveoli — hundreds of millions of tiny sacs whose combined surface area is enormous relative to the space they occupy. Alveolar walls are extraordinarily thin and wrapped in capillaries, so oxygen diffuses across into the blood and carbon dioxide diffuses out, both simply moving from higher to lower concentration. No active pumping is involved at that step; the whole design is about maximizing surface area and minimizing the distance a molecule has to travel. Understanding that explains why conditions that thicken alveolar walls, fill them with fluid, or destroy them all impair gas exchange in the same fundamental way.` },
        { heading: `What a pulse oximeter is telling you`, body: `Oxygen doesn't dissolve well in blood, so nearly all of it travels bound to hemoglobin inside red blood cells. A pulse oximeter clipped to a finger estimates what percentage of available hemoglobin is carrying oxygen — normally 95–100% in a healthy adult at sea level. Two things are worth knowing. The relationship between that percentage and actual oxygen levels is a curve, not a straight line: saturation stays near normal at first and then falls steeply, so a reading of 90% is a much bigger deal than the ten-point gap suggests. And it measures the percentage that's loaded, not how much hemoglobin there is — someone with severe anemia can read 99% while carrying far too little oxygen overall.` },
        { heading: `Why every health career needs this`, body: `A nurse checking vital signs, a paramedic assessing someone at a scene, a respiratory therapist managing a ventilator, a physical therapist deciding how hard a patient can safely exercise, a physician working through chest pain — all of them are reasoning about this same pair of systems, just at different depths and toward different decisions. This is a good illustration of why "pre-health" is a real shared foundation rather than a placeholder: the underlying content genuinely doesn't change based on which career you eventually pick.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Respiration handles gas exchange and circulation handles transport — they're one delivery operation, which is why symptoms rarely point cleanly to just one.`,
        `The heart is two pumps: a thinner-walled right side serving the lungs and a thicker-walled left side serving the whole body, with valves enforcing one-way flow.`,
        `Alveolar gas exchange works by passive diffusion across a very thin, very large surface — which is why anything that thickens or destroys that surface impairs it.`,
        `Pulse oximetry reads the percentage of hemoglobin carrying oxygen, follows a curve rather than a line, and can look normal in severe anemia.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: '9fxm85Fy4sQ', title: 'Circulatory & Respiratory Systems: Crash Course Biology #27', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },
  ex4l2: {
    readMins: 7,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Digestion as a disassembly line`, body: `The digestive tract is one continuous tube running from mouth to anus, and its job is to break food into pieces small enough to cross into the bloodstream. Mechanical breakdown starts with chewing; chemical breakdown starts with salivary enzymes acting on carbohydrates. The stomach adds acid and protein-digesting enzymes and holds food while mixing it. The small intestine does the overwhelming majority of both digestion and absorption, helped by enzymes from the pancreas and bile from the liver, across a surface area vastly expanded by folds and finger-like projections called villi. The large intestine reclaims water and forms stool. Each segment does something specific, which is why symptoms often point to a particular stretch of the tube.` },
        { heading: `The liver's outsized role`, body: `The liver is easy to overlook and does an unreasonable amount of work. It produces bile, which emulsifies fats so enzymes can act on them. It processes nutrients absorbed from the intestine before they reach the rest of the body. It stores glucose as glycogen and releases it when blood sugar falls. It manufactures most of the blood's proteins, including clotting factors. And it metabolizes drugs, alcohol, and other substances — which is why liver function matters so much to how medications are dosed. Almost no health career avoids the liver: it shows up in nutrition, pharmacology, emergency care, and chronic disease management alike.` },
        { heading: `The kidneys: filter, then take most of it back`, body: `Each kidney holds about a million filtering units called nephrons. Each one filters blood under pressure — pushing water and small molecules out of the bloodstream into a tubule — and then reclaims almost all of it as the filtrate travels along. The kidneys filter an enormous volume every day and return nearly all of it, producing roughly 1 to 2 liters of urine. The mental model worth keeping is "filter indiscriminately, then carefully take back what matters," because it explains the rest: how the kidney adjusts water retention when you're dehydrated, how it holds electrolytes in range, and how it participates in regulating blood pressure.` },
        { heading: `Fluid and electrolyte balance`, body: `Beyond removing waste, the kidneys keep the body's internal chemistry stable — water volume, sodium, potassium, calcium, and acid-base balance. These are defended within narrow limits because cells depend on those exact conditions to work. Potassium is the clearest example of why the margins are small: it directly affects the electrical activity of heart muscle, so both too much and too little are genuinely dangerous. This is the reason a basic metabolic panel — a simple blood test reporting kidney function and electrolytes — is one of the most frequently ordered tests in all of medicine, and something you'll see referenced constantly in any clinical setting.` },
        { heading: `Where these systems show up in real work`, body: `A dietitian reasoning about nutrient absorption, a pharmacist checking whether a drug is cleared by the liver or the kidneys, a nurse tracking a patient's fluid balance, a public health worker studying access to clean water and nutrition — all are working with this material at different altitudes. It also connects directly to things you already encounter: why dehydration makes you feel terrible, why alcohol affects the liver, why some medications say "take with food." Understanding these systems is genuinely useful literacy, not just a step toward a credential.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `The digestive tract is a segmented disassembly line, with the small intestine doing most digestion and absorption across a hugely expanded surface area.`,
        `The liver produces bile, processes absorbed nutrients, stores glucose, makes blood proteins, and metabolizes drugs — which is why it matters to dosing.`,
        `Kidneys filter indiscriminately and then reclaim nearly everything, producing 1–2 liters of urine a day from an enormous filtered volume.`,
        `Fluid and electrolyte balance is defended within narrow limits — potassium especially, because it directly affects cardiac electrical activity.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: 'yIoTRGfcMqM', title: 'Digestive System, Part 1: Crash Course Anatomy & Physiology #33', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },
  ex4l3: {
    readMins: 7,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Two communication systems, two speeds`, body: `Your body runs two separate signaling networks. The nervous system sends fast, precisely targeted electrical signals down specific pathways — the reason you pull your hand off a hot surface before you consciously register the heat. The endocrine system broadcasts chemical messages called hormones into the bloodstream, reaching every cell but affecting only those carrying the right receptor. That's much slower, operating over minutes to days, and it's suited to processes that unfold gradually: growth, metabolism, the stress response, reproduction. Neither system is better; they solve different problems, and most of physiology involves both working together.` },
        { heading: `How neurons carry a signal`, body: `A neuron transmits information as an electrical impulse traveling along its length, then converts it to a chemical signal at the junction with the next cell. At that junction — the synapse — the neuron releases neurotransmitters that cross a tiny gap and bind receptors on the next cell, either encouraging or discouraging it from firing. This chemical step is where an enormous amount of medicine acts: antidepressants, anesthetics, pain medications, and drugs for Parkinson's disease and epilepsy all work by modifying what happens at synapses. The nervous system is broadly divided into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (everything else), with the peripheral side further split into voluntary control and the automatic functions that run without your attention.` },
        { heading: `Hormones and receptors`, body: `A gland releases a hormone into the blood, and it circulates everywhere — but only cells with a matching receptor respond. That's what gives a broadcast system its specificity. Hormones come in two broad chemical types with a practical consequence: peptide hormones like insulin are water-soluble and bind receptors on the cell surface, while steroid hormones like cortisol are lipid-based and pass through the membrane to act inside the cell. This is exactly why insulin has to be injected rather than swallowed — it would be digested like any other protein — while steroid medications work fine as pills.` },
        { heading: `Negative feedback: the pattern under almost everything`, body: `Most regulation in the body works by negative feedback, the same logic as a thermostat: a change is detected, a response is triggered, and the response reduces the original change. Blood sugar rises after a meal, insulin is released, cells take up glucose, blood sugar falls, insulin release slows. Body temperature rises, sweating and blood vessel dilation increase heat loss, temperature falls. Once you recognize this pattern you'll see it everywhere in physiology, and it reframes a lot of disease: many common conditions, including diabetes and thyroid disorders, are best understood as a feedback loop that has broken at a specific point rather than as an arbitrary list of symptoms.` },
        { heading: `Why this connects to every health field`, body: `Mental health, neurology, endocrinology, and pharmacology obviously depend on this material — but so do less obvious areas. A physical therapist retraining movement after a stroke is working with the nervous system's capacity to adapt. A public health researcher studying chronic stress is working with the hormonal stress response. A nurse managing a diabetic patient is managing a broken feedback loop. And a psychologist and a physician are, from different directions, both working on how brain function produces behavior. This is probably the single best illustration of why the biological and behavioral sciences aren't really separate subjects.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `The nervous system signals fast and precisely through neurons; the endocrine system broadcasts hormones slowly and gets specificity from receptors.`,
        `Neurons convert electrical signals to chemical ones at synapses, which is where a large share of medications act.`,
        `Peptide hormones bind surface receptors and must be injected; steroid hormones enter the cell and work as pills.`,
        `Negative feedback is the control pattern under most of physiology, and many common diseases are best understood as a feedback loop broken at a specific point.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: 'eWHH9je2zG4', title: 'Endocrine System, Part 1 - Glands & Hormones: Crash Course Anatomy & Physiology #23', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },

  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // UNIT 5 — HOW MEDICINE ACTUALLY WORKS
  // The practical literacy that makes a first clinical exposure comprehensible.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ex5l1: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Medical terms are built, not memorized`, body: `Clinical language looks like an impossible vocabulary and is actually a small construction system. Most medical terms come apart into a root (the body part or system involved), sometimes a prefix (position, number, timing, or degree), and a suffix (the condition, procedure, or process). "Pericarditis" is peri- (around) + cardi (heart) + -itis (inflammation): inflammation of the tissue around the heart. "Nephrectomy" is nephr (kidney) + -ectomy (surgical removal). Once you know the pieces, you can decode terms you've never seen — which is a far better return on a few hours than trying to memorize a list.` },
        { heading: `The pieces worth learning first`, body: `A small set does most of the work. Suffixes: -itis (inflammation), -ectomy (removal), -otomy (cutting into), -ostomy (making an opening), -emia (blood condition), -pathy (disease), -algia (pain), -ology (study of), -scopy (looking inside), -graphy (recording). Prefixes: hyper- (above normal), hypo- (below normal), brady- (slow), tachy- (fast), poly- (many), dys- (difficult or abnormal), a-/an- (without), peri- (around), intra- (within), pre-/post- (before/after). Roots: cardi (heart), pulmon/pneum (lung), gastr (stomach), hepat (liver), nephr/ren (kidney), neur (nerve), derm (skin), oste (bone), my (muscle), hemat (blood).` },
        { heading: `Watch the pairs that differ by two letters`, body: `Several term pairs look nearly identical and mean opposite or genuinely different things, and they're worth flagging deliberately. Hypertension is high blood pressure; hypotension is low. Hyperglycemia is high blood sugar; hypoglycemia is low — and the treatments are opposites, which is why the distinction is not academic. Bradycardia is a slow heart rate; tachycardia is fast. A colotomy is cutting into the colon; a colostomy is creating an opening from it to the surface. These are the terms that get misread under pressure, which is exactly why learning them as pairs rather than individually is worth doing.` },
        { heading: `Directions, positions, and whose left is left`, body: `Clinical descriptions assume a shared reference called anatomical position: standing upright, facing forward, arms at the sides with palms turned forward. From there: superior and inferior mean toward the head and feet, anterior and posterior mean front and back, medial and lateral mean toward and away from the body's midline, and proximal and distal mean closer to and farther from where a limb attaches — your knee is proximal to your ankle. One rule catches nearly everyone: left and right always mean the patient's left and right, not yours. That's why anatomical diagrams and X-rays appear mirrored from what you'd expect.` },
        { heading: `Why this pays off immediately`, body: `This is the highest-return few hours you can spend before any first clinical exposure. Walk into a shadowing day without it and most of what's said will pass by; walk in with it and you can follow a conversation, read along with a note, and ask a question that shows you were actually tracking — which, practically, is also what makes someone willing to have you back. And because every health profession uses the same construction system, none of this is wasted if your interests shift later.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Medical terms decompose into root, prefix, and suffix — learning the pieces lets you decode terms you've never encountered.`,
        `A short list of common affixes and roots covers a large share of clinical language you'll actually hear.`,
        `Pairs like hyper-/hypo-, brady-/tachy-, and -otomy/-ostomy differ by a letter or two and mean genuinely different things.`,
        `Directional terms assume anatomical position, and left and right always mean the patient's — which is why anatomical images look mirrored.`,
      ],
    },
  },
  ex5l2: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `The five vital signs`, body: `Vital signs are the quickest check on whether someone's basic physiology is working, and they're the same five almost everywhere. Body temperature: normally around 36.1–37.2°C (97–99°F) in an adult, varying with time of day and where it's measured. Heart rate: 60–100 beats per minute at rest. Respiratory rate: 12–20 breaths per minute. Blood pressure: under 120/80 mmHg is considered normal by current US guidelines. Oxygen saturation: 95–100% in a healthy adult at sea level. Pain is often tracked alongside these, though it's reported by the patient rather than measured by an instrument — a real difference in what kind of data it is.` },
        { heading: `The numbers only mean something in context`, body: `A resting heart rate of 52 could indicate a problem, or it could mean the person is a trained distance runner. A temperature of 37.8°C means something different in someone who just finished a workout than in someone lying in bed. Blood pressure readings vary with anxiety, caffeine, a full bladder, whether the arm was supported, and even whether the person was talking during the measurement. This is why nobody in healthcare treats a single reading as a verdict: measurements are interpreted against the person, the situation, and — most importantly — their own previous numbers.` },
        { heading: `What "normal range" actually means`, body: `Lab reference ranges are more slippery than they look. A range is typically built so that about the middle 95% of a healthy reference population falls inside it — which means roughly 1 in 20 completely healthy people will fall outside any given range by construction. Run fifteen different tests on a perfectly healthy person and the odds that at least one comes back flagged are better than even. Ranges also differ between labs, between age groups, and during pregnancy. So a flagged value is a reason to look more carefully, not a diagnosis — and this is genuinely useful to know for reading your own lab results, not just someone else's.` },
        { heading: `Trends beat single measurements`, body: `The single most useful idea in reading health data is that direction matters more than position. A heart rate of 96 is unremarkable on its own; a heart rate of 96 in someone who has been at 68 all day is a real signal. A weight that's stable tells a different story than the same weight after a five-pound gain in three days. Hospitals use early warning systems built specifically on this principle, aggregating small changes across several vital signs to flag patients who are deteriorating before any single number looks alarming. Numbers describe a state; trends describe where things are heading.` },
        { heading: `The two most common blood tests`, body: `You'll hear these referenced constantly in any clinical setting, so it's worth knowing what they are. A complete blood count (CBC) reports the three cell lines in blood: red cells (relevant to anemia), white cells (relevant to infection and immune function), and platelets (relevant to clotting). A basic metabolic panel (BMP) reports kidney function, electrolytes like sodium and potassium, and blood glucose. You are not expected to interpret either diagnostically — but knowing what each one is asking about means that when someone mentions "her CBC came back," you know roughly what part of the body is under discussion.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Adult reference points: temperature ~36.1–37.2°C, heart rate 60–100/min, respiratory rate 12–20/min, blood pressure under 120/80, oxygen saturation 95–100%.`,
        `Vital signs are interpreted against the person and situation — a low heart rate in an athlete and in a patient on a new medication mean different things.`,
        `Reference ranges capture about the middle 95% of healthy people, so roughly 1 in 20 healthy results falls outside by construction.`,
        `Trends carry more information than single readings, which is the principle behind hospital early-warning systems.`,
      ],
    },
  },
  ex5l3: {
    readMins: 7,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Two layers of immune defense`, body: `Your immune system works in two layers. Innate immunity is fast and non-specific: physical barriers like skin and mucus, plus general-purpose defenders like macrophages and neutrophils that attack anything recognized as foreign, responding within minutes to hours. Adaptive immunity is slower to start but far more precise: it learns to recognize a specific pathogen's molecular markers, called antigens, and mounts a tailored response that takes days to ramp up the first time. Every infection involves both, and how quickly and effectively they engage is a large part of why some infections clear on their own and others need treatment.` },
        { heading: `Antibodies and immune memory`, body: `B cells respond to a specific antigen by producing antibodies — Y-shaped proteins that bind that exact antigen and either neutralize it directly or tag it for destruction. The crucial part is what happens afterward: some cells activated during an infection persist as memory cells, ready to mount a much faster and stronger response if the same pathogen appears again. This is the entire mechanism behind immunity after an infection, and it's also exactly what vaccination exploits — training the immune system on an antigen so the memory exists without having to survive the actual disease first. It's one of the more elegant pieces of biology and one of the most consequential in public health.` },
        { heading: `The chain of infection`, body: `Infection control is built on a simple model: for an infection to spread, six links have to connect. There must be an infectious agent, living in a reservoir (a person, a surface, water), with a way out (a portal of exit), a mode of transmission (touch, droplets, airborne particles), a way in (a portal of entry), and a susceptible host at the end. Break any one link and transmission fails. The reason this framework is taught first is that it shows immediately where effort pays off: you can't eliminate every germ or make everyone immune on demand, but you can interrupt transmission — which is what handwashing, gloves, masks, and cleaning surfaces all do.` },
        { heading: `What that means for you in a clinical setting`, body: `If you volunteer or shadow in a healthcare setting, infection control applies to you exactly as it does to staff. Hand hygiene before and after any patient contact, and after touching anything in a patient's environment, is not optional and is genuinely the highest-impact thing you'll do. Alcohol-based hand rub is faster and generally more effective for routine use, but soap and water is required when hands are visibly dirty and in certain specific situations, because alcohol doesn't remove soil or kill some spore-forming bacteria. Follow whatever precautions are posted for a room without needing them explained, stay home when you're sick — patients in a hospital are frequently far more vulnerable than you are — and keep your own vaccinations current, which most volunteer programs will require anyway.` },
        { heading: `Why immunity connects biology to public health`, body: `This is where individual biology becomes population science. Immune memory in one person is biology; immune memory across a population is epidemiology, and the reason vaccination programs can protect people who can't themselves be vaccinated. Outbreak investigation, antibiotic resistance, hospital infection rates, and vaccination policy are all this same material at a larger scale. If the population-level version is the part you find more interesting than the cellular mechanism, that's real information about which direction in health suits you — and worth noticing rather than dismissing.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Innate immunity is fast and non-specific; adaptive immunity is slower but targets specific antigens and leaves memory behind.`,
        `Antibodies from B cells neutralize or tag antigens, and memory cells are the mechanism behind both post-infection immunity and vaccination.`,
        `The chain of infection has six links, and interrupting transmission is the link you can most reliably break.`,
        `Hand hygiene applies to volunteers and students exactly as it does to staff — alcohol rub routinely, soap and water for visible soil and certain organisms.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: 'GIJK3dwCWCw', title: 'Immune System, Part 1: Crash Course Anatomy & Physiology #45', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },

  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // UNIT 6 — HEALTH CAREERS UP CLOSE
  // Training lengths below are typical US pathways after high school and are
  // deliberately given as ranges; program structures vary and change, so these
  // lessons point at what to verify rather than presenting fixed figures.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ex6l1: {
    readMins: 7,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `The question that sorts clinical roles fastest`, body: `Health careers are usually introduced as a list of job titles, which is close to useless for choosing between them. A far better organizing question is: who carries final responsibility for the diagnosis and treatment decision? That single axis separates roles more cleanly than salary, setting, or prestige. Physicians carry it fully. PAs and NPs carry substantial decision-making authority within structures that vary by state. Nurses carry enormous responsibility for assessment, monitoring, and care delivery within a plan set collaboratively. Therapists and technologists carry deep responsibility within a specific domain. None of these is "higher" in any meaningful sense — they're different relationships to the decision.` },
        { heading: `The diagnose-and-prescribe roles`, body: `Physicians (MD or DO) complete four years of undergraduate study, four years of medical school, and a residency of three to seven or more years depending on specialty — the longest path here, with the broadest scope and deepest training. Physician assistants complete a bachelor's plus substantial patient-care hours, then a master's program of roughly 27 months, and are trained on the medical model as generalists who can change specialty without retraining. Nurse practitioners become registered nurses first, then complete a graduate degree, trained on the nursing model with an earlier population focus. All three diagnose, treat, and prescribe; how much independence each has varies by state law.` },
        { heading: `Nursing and the direct-care roles`, body: `Registered nurses enter through either a two-year associate degree or a four-year bachelor's, both leading to the same NCLEX licensing exam. Nurses spend more time with patients than anyone else in the system, which makes them the ones who notice change first — a genuinely central clinical role, not a supporting one. Below and alongside that sit roles with shorter training and real responsibility: certified nursing assistants, medical assistants, and paramedics, who complete training measured in months to about two years and do hands-on work that a great many people use as an entry point into healthcare while deciding what's next.` },
        { heading: `The therapy and technical specialists`, body: `Physical therapists now complete a doctorate (DPT), typically three years after a bachelor's, and focus on movement, strength, and mobility. Occupational therapists complete a master's or doctorate and focus on function in daily life — dressing, working, self-care — which is the distinction people most often get wrong between the two. Respiratory therapists complete an associate or bachelor's degree and manage breathing, oxygen delivery, and ventilators, making them central in ICUs and emergency departments. Radiologic technologists, sonographers, surgical technologists, and medical laboratory scientists each own a specific technical domain, with training typically running two to four years.` },
        { heading: `How to actually use this map`, body: `Two things are worth noticing. First, training length varies from under a year to over a decade, and the shortest path to a job you'd dislike is still a long career doing it — so speed is a poor primary criterion. Second, several of these roles overlap far more in daily work than their different titles suggest, particularly PAs and NPs. The productive next step is not picking from this list but noticing which two or three descriptions made you want to know more, and then arranging to see those specifically. That's what the shadowing lesson in the next unit is for.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `"Who carries final responsibility for the decision?" sorts clinical roles more usefully than salary, setting, or prestige.`,
        `Physician, PA, and NP all diagnose, treat, and prescribe, reached through very different training routes with independence varying by state law.`,
        `RNs enter through a two- or four-year degree and are the people who notice patient change first; CNA, MA, and paramedic roles offer much shorter entry points.`,
        `PT focuses on movement while OT focuses on function in daily life — the distinction people most often get wrong — and technical roles own specific domains in 2–4 years of training.`,
      ],
    },
  },
  ex6l2: {
    readMins: 7,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `The part of healthcare nobody shows you`, body: `Nearly every depiction of healthcare a student encounters is set at a bedside, which leaves out an enormous share of the actual field. Laboratory science, epidemiology, health informatics, hospital administration, biomedical research, health policy, medical device engineering, and public health together employ a very large portion of the health workforce — and most students have never had a single one described to them. That's not because these roles are marginal. It's because they're invisible in the settings and stories students are exposed to, which is a poor reason to leave them out of a decision this consequential.` },
        { heading: `Public health: working on populations`, body: `Public health asks a different question than clinical medicine: not "what's wrong with this patient?" but "why is this happening to this community, and what would prevent it?" The field spans epidemiology (tracking how disease moves through populations), biostatistics, environmental health, health education, and policy. Its signature feature is leverage — water fluoridation, vaccination programs, seatbelt requirements, and tobacco control each changed outcomes for millions of people, far beyond what any clinician reaches individually. Its signature frustration is invisibility: when public health works, nothing happens, and it's genuinely hard to get credit for an outbreak that didn't occur.` },
        { heading: `Research, laboratory science, and informatics`, body: `Biomedical research is where treatments come from, and it's slow, uncertain work that suits some people enormously and frustrates others — worth testing early rather than assuming. Medical laboratory scientists run and interpret the tests clinical decisions depend on; a large share of diagnoses rest on work done in a lab by someone the patient never meets. Health informatics sits between clinicians and technology, designing and managing the electronic records, decision support, and data systems that healthcare now runs on — a field that barely existed a few decades ago and is now unavoidable, and where poor design is a documented cause of both clinician burnout and medical error.` },
        { heading: `Administration, policy, and the systems layer`, body: `Someone decides how a hospital is staffed, how patients flow through an emergency department, what a health system can afford, and how insurance coverage works. Health administrators and health economists do that, and the quality of those decisions determines whether clinicians can actually do their jobs. Health policy operates one level up, at the government and organizational level where the rules are set. These roles get dismissed as "not really medicine," which is both wrong and expensive — a badly run system produces bad outcomes no matter how skilled the clinicians inside it are.` },
        { heading: `Testing interest when you can't shadow`, body: `Most of these roles are harder to shadow than a clinical one, but not harder to explore. Informational interviews work remarkably well — a specific, polite request for twenty minutes to ask someone about their job is granted far more often than students expect, and adults generally enjoy talking about their work. Volunteering at a local health department, a health fair, or a community health organization gives direct exposure. And for the quantitative roles, doing an actual small project — analyzing a public health dataset, building something with real data — tells you more about whether you like the work than any amount of reading. None of that requires clinical access, which is exactly why it's worth doing early.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Lab science, epidemiology, informatics, administration, research, and policy employ a large share of the health workforce and are almost never shown to students.`,
        `Public health's defining feature is population-level leverage; its defining frustration is that success is invisible by nature.`,
        `Informatics and lab science are quantitatively demanding — "non-clinical" describes who you interact with, not how rigorous the work is.`,
        `Informational interviews, health department volunteering, and a real data project test interest in these roles without requiring clinical access.`,
      ],
    },
    video: { ytId: '5aww-Bpgkf4', title: 'What is Public Health? Crash Course Public Health #1', channel: 'Crash Course' },
  },
  ex6l3: {
    readMins: 7,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Why salary is the worst variable to choose on`, body: `Salary is the number students weight most and the one that predicts long-term satisfaction least, for a specific reason: roles with similar pay can involve completely different lives. Two careers paying comparably might differ by night shifts versus daytime clinic hours, a decade of training versus two years, substantial debt versus none, and crisis-driven work versus longitudinal relationships. Those differences shape a life; the salary difference mostly shapes a spreadsheet. Reliable pay data by occupation is publicly available from the US Bureau of Labor Statistics and worth consulting — just not first, and not alone.` },
        { heading: `Counting the full cost of training`, body: `Training length has two costs, and students consistently see only one. The visible cost is tuition. The invisible one is opportunity cost: years spent training are years not spent earning, saving, and building a life outside school. A path that takes seven additional years costs those years of income and compounding, plus whatever life events happen during them. This is not an argument against long training paths — many people find them entirely worth it — but it's an argument for making the comparison with both costs visible rather than only the tuition line. Debt load in particular varies enormously between health professions and follows people for decades.` },
        { heading: `Schedule and shift structure`, body: `This deserves far more weight than it usually gets. Some health careers involve decades of night shifts, rotating schedules, weekends, and on-call weeks; others involve essentially none. Shift work is a well-documented health exposure in its own right, with measurable effects on sleep, cardiovascular and metabolic health, and family life. It also determines something more mundane and more constant: whether you're reliably present for evenings, weekends, and the ordinary rhythm of the people you live with. If you already know something about how you function without sleep, that's real data about which paths suit you.` },
        { heading: `Burnout is about conditions, not credentials`, body: `Research on clinician burnout consistently points to workload, control over one's own work, administrative burden, and organizational support rather than to which credential someone holds. The same job title can be sustainable in a well-run organization and corrosive in a badly run one. This matters for how you use any career comparison: burnout rates reported for a profession describe the conditions those professionals typically work in, not something inherent to the work itself. It also means the choice of employer and setting will eventually matter as much as the choice of profession — which is worth knowing now, so it isn't a surprise later.` },
        { heading: `Using tradeoffs rather than looking for a winner`, body: `There is no dominant option here. Every one of these paths trades something real for something else: autonomy for training length, income for time, breadth for depth, excitement for predictability. The useful question is not "which health career is best?" — that question has no answer — but "which set of costs would I genuinely accept for the next thirty years?" That's answerable, and it's personal. It's also why exploring several roles before committing is a strategy rather than indecision: you can't weigh tradeoffs you haven't seen.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Similar-paying careers can differ enormously in schedule, autonomy, debt, and daily work — salary predicts satisfaction poorly.`,
        `Training cost includes opportunity cost: years not spent earning and saving, plus debt that follows people for decades.`,
        `Shift work is a documented health exposure and determines your everyday availability, so it deserves serious weight rather than a footnote.`,
        `Burnout tracks workload, autonomy, and organizational support rather than credential — so setting and employer eventually matter as much as profession.`,
      ],
    },
  },

  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // UNIT 7 — BUILDING YOUR FOUNDATION NOW
  // Deliberately pathway-agnostic: everything in this unit is worth doing
  // regardless of which health career the student eventually chooses.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ex7l1: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `The shared backbone`, body: `The genuinely useful thing about being undecided is that the high school preparation for essentially every health career is the same: biology, chemistry, physics, and mathematics through at least statistics. Anatomy and physiology, psychology, and health science are strong additions where your school offers them. This is not a coincidence — these subjects recur as prerequisites across medicine, nursing, PA, pharmacy, physical and occupational therapy, and public health because they're what the college-level coursework in all of those fields assumes. Building this backbone costs you nothing in optionality and keeps every door open, which is exactly the right strategy when you haven't chosen yet.` },
        { heading: `Rigor is judged relative to your school`, body: `Admissions readers evaluate the rigor of your coursework in the context of what your school actually offers. A student taking the most demanding science sequence available at a school with three AP options is not disadvantaged against a student at a school with twenty. If your school has limited offerings, dual enrollment or community college courses are a well-recognized way to add depth, and worth asking a counselor about. What isn't useful is comparing your transcript to one from a school with completely different resources — that's not the comparison being made.` },
        { heading: `The AP credit question, stated honestly`, body: `A strong AP score can earn college credit, and for health-career students that's genuinely double-edged. Many programs — medical, PA, and nursing among them — either don't accept AP credit toward prerequisite requirements or expect additional upper-level coursework in the subject instead. Beyond that, skipping introductory biology or chemistry means starting in a harder course without the foundation your classmates just built, and giving up a course where you'd likely have earned a strong grade. The sensible approach: take the AP courses, because the preparation is real, and decide about applying the credit later when you know a specific program's policy. That decision doesn't have to be made now, and shouldn't be.` },
        { heading: `Prerequisite chains and the four-year map`, body: `Courses chain. Reaching AP Chemistry as a junior generally requires regular chemistry as a sophomore, which requires a certain math level. Getting to statistics or calculus requires the algebra and precalculus sequence beneath it. Students who choose one year at a time frequently discover as juniors that a door quietly closed as freshmen. Sketching a rough four-year map — one you'll revise, not a commitment — surfaces those chains while there's still time to act. Your school counselor has done this hundreds of times and is genuinely the right person to check the map against.` },
        { heading: `Rigor versus doing well`, body: `The most common self-inflicted problem in health-career planning is an overloaded schedule that produces mediocre grades and real burnout. Rigor matters, but so do grades, and so does having enough time left for the sustained activities and clinical exposure that also matter — and that, more importantly, are how you find out what you actually want. A useful test before adding another advanced course: would taking it still leave you able to do well in everything, and to keep doing the things outside class you care about? If the honest answer is no, that course is costing more than it adds.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `Biology, chemistry, physics, and math through statistics form a backbone shared by essentially every health career — building it costs no optionality.`,
        `Course rigor is judged relative to what your school offers, and dual enrollment is a recognized way to add depth where offerings are limited.`,
        `Many health programs don't accept AP credit for prerequisites — take the courses for the preparation and decide about the credit later.`,
        `Prerequisite chains close doors quietly, so sketch a revisable four-year map with a counselor rather than choosing one year at a time.`,
      ],
    },
  },
  ex7l2: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `What's actually available in high school`, body: `More than most students realize. HOSA – Future Health Professionals runs chapters at many high schools with leadership roles and competitive events across health topics. Many districts offer health science academies, career and technical education pathways, or dual-enrollment health courses that carry college credit. Beyond school: CPR/BLS certification is a single short course and is frequently a prerequisite for volunteering. Hospital teen volunteer programs exist in most areas. And two certifications — Certified Nursing Assistant and Emergency Medical Technician — lead to actual paid, hands-on work. That range means there's a realistic next step regardless of how much time you have.` },
        { heading: `Which ones produce countable hours`, body: `This distinction matters more than it appears, because health programs count different things differently. Shadowing is observation — genuinely valuable, often required, but you're not responsible for anything. Volunteering usually involves a real if limited task. Direct patient care means hands-on responsibility for part of someone's care, and it's the category PA and nursing programs weight most heavily. CNA and EMT are the two high school certifications that most reliably produce direct patient-care hours, because both lead to roles where you're actually doing the care. If a specific program's requirements might matter to you later, log enough detail now — role, tasks, setting — that you can categorize the hours correctly against whatever definition applies.` },
        { heading: `CNA and EMT, specifically`, body: `CNA training is state-approved and combines classroom instruction with supervised clinical hours; federal rules set a minimum training length and many states require more. After a competency exam you're listed on your state's nurse aide registry and can work in long-term care, hospitals, and home health. EMT involves a longer course covering emergency assessment and treatment, leading to National Registry (NREMT) certification and then state licensure, opening work with ambulance services and event medical teams. Age requirements vary by state — many programs allow training at 16 or 17 while requiring 18 for certification or employment — so your own state's board is the only reliable source on what applies to you.` },
        { heading: `Checking a program is real before you pay`, body: `This field contains genuine training and it contains programs that take money and confer nothing. The verification is specific. For CNA: confirm the program is approved by your state's nurse aide registry or health department, because only a state-approved program makes you registry-eligible. For EMT: confirm it leads to NREMT eligibility. Be skeptical of any online-only "certification" for a hands-on clinical skill, since supervised clinical hours cannot be completed through a screen. A certificate that doesn't lead to registry or NREMT eligibility isn't a credential, whatever it looks like. The state board's website, not the program's marketing, is the source of truth.` },
        { heading: `Cost, access, and doing this without money`, body: `Certification programs cost money, and that's a real barrier rather than a footnote. Several routes reduce or remove it: some high schools offer CNA training as a CTE course at no cost, some employers — particularly long-term care facilities — sponsor CNA training in exchange for a work commitment, and some volunteer fire departments and ambulance services train EMTs for free in exchange for service hours. Hospital volunteering costs nothing but time, and HOSA membership is inexpensive. If cost is the constraint, ask a counselor about the funded pathways in your area before concluding the option is closed — they're more common than they're advertised.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `HOSA, health science academies, dual enrollment, CPR certification, hospital volunteering, CNA, and EMT are all realistically available in high school.`,
        `Shadowing, volunteering, and direct patient care are counted differently by health programs — CNA and EMT most reliably produce patient-care hours.`,
        `Verify state approval for CNA and NREMT eligibility for EMT before paying; online-only certification for a hands-on skill is a warning sign.`,
        `Funded routes exist — CTE courses, employer-sponsored CNA training, volunteer EMS — so ask a counselor before ruling an option out on cost.`,
      ],
    },
  },
  ex7l3: {
    readMins: 6,
    article: {
      sections: [
        { heading: `Breadth beats depth when you're still deciding`, body: `The standard advice about extracurriculars — depth over breadth — is right in general and wrong for this specific purpose. If you already know which career you want, sustained hours in that setting are the strongest thing you can build. If you don't, spending all your exposure in one setting means you'll choose based on the only thing you've seen, which is a poor way to make a decision this large. Shadowing two or three genuinely different roles teaches you something no amount of reading can: what the day actually feels like. The right sequence is breadth first, then depth once you've found the thing worth going deep on.` },
        { heading: `Arranging it as a high schooler`, body: `Personal connections are, realistically, the main route in. A family doctor or dentist, a relative or family friend in healthcare, a neighbor who's a nurse or therapist — these are the arrangements that actually happen, and a parent or guardian is usually involved in setting them up. Smaller practices and clinics often have more flexibility than large hospital systems, which typically have stricter liability and privacy policies for minors. Beyond clinical settings, physical therapy clinics, pharmacies, dental offices, veterinary clinics, and local health departments are all more accessible than a hospital and each shows you a genuinely different kind of work. Expect some no's; they're about policy, not about you.` },
        { heading: `What to actually pay attention to`, body: `The dramatic case of the day is the least useful thing you'll see. What tells you about fit is the ordinary texture: how much of the day is direct patient contact versus documentation and administrative work — this surprises nearly everyone, since charting takes up a substantial share of most clinicians' time. How much of the work is solo versus team-based. Whether encounters last five minutes or an hour. How the person handles uncertainty, and how they handle a conversation that isn't going well. And, honestly, whether you were interested at hour four or checking the time. That last one is real data.` },
        { heading: `Confidentiality, without exception`, body: `Everything you observe about a specific patient is confidential. No discussing identifiable details with friends or family, nothing on social media, no photos, no exceptions — and this holds even for details that seem harmless, because you're not the one positioned to judge what identifies someone. This isn't a formality: violating it can end a shadowing arrangement immediately and reflects on the person who agreed to host you. What you can and should record are your own observations and reflections, with no patient-identifying detail whatsoever. That's the line, and it's the same line professionals work under.` },
        { heading: `Logging it so it means something later`, body: `After each experience, record the date, setting, role observed, hours, and a few sentences on what you noticed and what surprised you — in your Portfolio's Clinical Hours panel, which exists for this. Two reasons. First, a record built as you go is accurate and specific; one reconstructed two years later is vague and incomplete. Second, and more useful right now: writing down what you noticed forces you to actually notice. Varied exposure logged deliberately reads as genuine exploration rather than drift — and more importantly, reading back over several entries from different settings is often what makes the pattern in your own reactions visible to you.` },
      ],
      keyTakeaways: [
        `While you're still deciding, breadth beats depth — seeing only one setting means choosing based on the only thing you've seen.`,
        `Personal connections and smaller practices are the realistic route in, and PT clinics, pharmacies, dental offices, and health departments are more accessible than hospitals.`,
        `Watch the ordinary texture — documentation load, encounter length, solo versus team, how uncertainty is handled — not the dramatic case.`,
        `Confidentiality is absolute, and logging reflections as you go both preserves detail and forces you to actually notice what you saw.`,
      ],
    },
  },
};
